## Required gates

- **Distribution terms:** G01 records owner-approved v2 source/binary terms,
  Academy redistribution permission and every framework-derived import's
  disposition under [distribution.md](distribution.md). No extraction before
  clearance; C26 verifies notices/terms against exact shipped inventory.

- **Provenance:** evaluate each direct import's actual license/notice and allowed
  usage. cdot-framework is not blanket MIT and its billing-retention condition
  cannot be erased by renaming. Prefer independently implemented patterns where
  direct copying is unsuitable. Academy sharing is owner-directed; establish
  package ownership and third-party notices rather than inventing a license.
- **Academy packaging:** React19/Tailwind4/Vite7 are observed in Academy, while
  the framework uses React18/Tailwind3. Select a compatible pinned desktop stack
  and one shared presentation source. The existing UI package re-exports app
  source and is not a ready independently reusable full learner app.
- **Shared-source changes:** propose the smallest extraction that supports actual
  web/desktop consumers. Keep Academy web behavior and hosted contracts stable;
  run both projects' affected tests. Pin the reviewed shared revision/package
  before completing its consuming desktop chunk.
- **SDK scan:** ec6230d2's accepted subset supplies synchronous scan. It requires
  explicit scan credentials; management OAuth is separate. Accepted Linux
  reports do not establish macOS ARM64 support. Qualify the native compiler/TLS
  dependencies, runtime integration, scanner contracts, cancellation, sanitized
  errors, and unknown outcomes in C02–C03.
- **SDK administration:** 32cbcb6d's accepted Phase4 is a candidate input for
  C08–C12. Map every actual operation, option, pagination bound, and error to
  accepted source/tests. SDK family labels are insufficient proof of parity.
- **SDK later families:** Phase5 Red Team/Model and Phase6 Gateway were developing
  or pending during inspection. Their exact accepted pins are prerequisites to
  C13/C16–C18. Do not consume unfinished work or substitute mocks as completion.
  Stable v2 must qualify the SDK subset it actually uses, including native/live
  evidence; it need not claim every unrelated SDK method is complete.
- **Harness:** retain the desktop-capable native engine/protocol after source and
  package checks. Do not substitute an older canonical checkout or npm launcher.
  Rebuild/reseal v2 resources to omit Node/managed CLI where proven unnecessary;
  old v1 resource hashes do not attest the changed v2 bundle. Preserve inference
  and MCP routing/context/tool restrictions.
- **Native host:** select pinned Tauri2/Rust/toolchain dependencies compatible
  with macOS ARM64 and the chosen SDK. Resolve one application-owned async
  runtime strategy; do not create an independent runtime per request.
- **Identity:** specify independent v2 bundle/display/data/Keychain identities in
  G01. Test v1/v2 coexistence, reset, uninstall, import, and rollback. Do not
  silently reuse a v1 namespace or change the learner's remote account.
- **Live environment:** identify designated test tenants/accounts, scopes,
  reversible resources, budgets, and cleanup/readback before C25 execution.
  Prod configuration discovery is not mutation authorization. Missing access is
  a real dependency gap; never report skipped tests as passed.
- **Hosted OAuth registrations:** G01 maps reuse/new-client/redirect/scope needs
  for Academy device authorization, Harness company SSO and MCP. Responsible
  Academy/Gateway/IdP operators own authorized staging/configuration changes and
  deployment. V2 identity alone neither proves a new registration is required
  nor authorizes production changes. Resolve before the consuming live tests.
- **Signing:** identify an authorized Developer ID, provisioning/entitlement
  requirements, notarization access, and a native acceptance host. Access secrets
  only at use time through approved storage, never committed plan variables.

## Official host references

Tauri supports Rust commands and async messages between presentation and host;
the selected command/module interface still requires G01 design and review.
[Calling Rust](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/calling-rust/)

Window/WebView capability grants require deliberate scoping; renderer grants do
not replace Rust's application authority checks.
[Capabilities](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/)

DMG distribution and macOS signing are separate from successful compilation.
[DMG packaging](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/dmg/),
[macOS signing/notarization](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).
# 1. Native operation authority owns coordination

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D008,D009,D013,D014,D016,D019.

V1 already has deep domain/request modules, but GPUI callers still coordinate
slots, protected-key IO and ordered effects. React must not inherit that authority.

Choose a principal-bound Rust actor with dispatch(Intent) and observe(ViewScope).
Keep feature transitions domain-owned inside one initial core crate. Native
bootstrap injects adapters and creates operator/Harness handles; JSON cannot
select the caller principal. Finite domain instructions exclude arbitrary SDK
method strings and any Harness Apply capability.

The interface includes atomic admission/context retirement, per-source supersession,
separate read/review/write/stream domains, priority/deadline/rate-limit semantics,
bounded queues, bounded delivery-sequence deduplication with safe session rotation,
single-use settlement and truthful Remote outcome. No remote IO
or blocking vault call occurs while the authoritative transition is locked.
Admitted work runs on the host's one async runtime; native adapters execute effects.

React owns raw editor text/undo. Rust owns immutable captured drafts/reviews.
Review/Apply/Save/Send capture fresh input in their actual production handlers;
Apply compares fresh input with the stored reviewed operation and executes only
that stored operation. Invalid/lossy input, changed context/draft/resource or a
consumed review refuses dispatch. Never rely only on a debounced revision.
RequestApply uses a native operator confirmation; actual Apply completion is
private, single-use and revalidated after the modal editor/context lease.
Every scoped intent/observation carries native-issued context and principal
admission. Async vault completions are generation/lease-bound; obsolete writes
receive orphan cleanup rather than reactivating credentials. Explicit one-time
secret reveal/copy stays in a scoped transient native dialog, never snapshots.

Alternatives considered under the design-it-twice skill:

| Design | Depth/locality | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| Actor dispatch/observe | Callers learn one authority seam; context and settlement stay together | Finite unions and observation backpressure need discipline |
| Typed scoped handles | Strong domain methods and revoked scopes; flexible internal composition | More handles/methods and IPC registries for callers to coordinate |
| Task-oriented scan board | Trivial first scan workflow | Public task surface grows across all parity features |

Recommend the actor with domain-specific types and a small generated presentation
facade. Retain the alternatives' explicit scoped validation and effortless scan
usage internally. Do not combine all their public surfaces or add pass-through
modules merely to obtain two method names.

Two real execution adapters (SDK/scripted), storage adapters (Keychain/session/
fault-injectable) and clocks justify internal seams. The interface is the test
surface: switch/replace/cancel while queued/dispatched, old/duplicate reply,
fresh-input races, double Apply, denied Harness Apply, unknown outcome/no replay,
priority/deadline/rate-limit containment and observation resynchronization.

See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for ownership/wire details.
No app code has been written and no native/SDK test is claimed by this ADR.
# 2. Share Academy presentation through explicit composition

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D004,D005,D006,D007,D010.

The current UI package re-exports web app source. Shell palette/mobile navigation
and theme persistence require tRPC/Clerk/router context; the authenticated web
entry also starts learner and manager behavior. A whole-app import would spread
that authority into unrelated desktop features.

Keep one Academy-owned presentation implementation. Create explicit provider-free
controls/theme/shell exports while preserving the existing tooling entry. Both
Academy web and desktop consume the extracted source. Composition adapters supply
brand/navigation/account/header/palette/mobile slots; the shared module owns
visual semantics, responsive layout and focus. No universal plugin framework.

Desktop global theme is an app preference stored through Rust, not hosted
settings.update. Academy web keeps its learner-account theme behavior. Learner
notifications, gamification and account effects mount only in Academy composition.

Extract learner views separately when C05/C06 require them. Retain React Query/
tRPC hooks behind a transport/navigation provider; type-only learner contracts
build without the Express/DB runtime. Web uses existing HTTP authentication;
desktop uses a terminating link into the allowlisted Rust Academy transport.
Tokens stay native. Hosted learning/access/grading/award truth and client-safe
bundle/draft contracts remain authoritative. Never bundle raw answer content.

Directly embedding the authenticated web root is rejected because its interface
contains unrelated account/manager assumptions. Maintaining a desktop UI copy
is rejected because appearance and learner behavior would diverge.

Tests render shared presentation without hosted identity/query providers, run
both consumers' regressions, verify native keyboard/focus/themes, and prove no
learner mutation/gamification effect from general desktop screens. Learner tests
cover native link envelopes/allowlist, account cache retirement and hosted authority.

Dependency/source/license pins are extraction prerequisites. This ADR changes
no Academy checkout, package or hosted registration. See
[selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).
# 3. Retain native Harness and own scoped identity in Rust

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D010,D011,D014–D016,D021–D023.

Desktop-server is already a Rust engine. V1 host workers and its package manifest
still require Node/managed CLI. Preserve engine depth and replace those host
requirements, rather than rewrite the agent. Source inspection alone does not
establish that a newly sealed installed bundle works without the old resources.

Native lifecycle owns sealed executable selection, bounded stdin bootstrap,
protocol supervision, process cleanup, scoped grants and protected history.
React only presents lifecycle state. Grant/tenant/project changes retire the
run; approvals bind actual call items. Preserve existing project/process/tool
restrictions, Gateway-only inference/MCP and no engine Apply.

Identity shares native discovery/HTTP/validation/storage mechanics while retaining
Academy learner, Harness inference and MCP grant policies. They are distinct
identities with distinct sign-out/refresh/context effects. Use public-client
PKCE/device flows and a no-redirect native transport; never embed a client secret
or trust renderer-selected issuer/token destinations.

Host signature/JWKS validation is a deliberate stricter v2 requirement; v1
omitted it. Validate ID/access token types according to their actual contracts,
not by decoding arbitrary JWT claims. Rotation/expiry/sleep/revocation fail closed.
Same-identity token refresh restarts the one-shot-bootstrap engine and restores
compatible in-session history; it re-confirms workspace-write permission and
never replays remote/tool effects. Declining workspace-write confirmation keeps
the draft/cancels Send without restart. Any changed ContextBinding (including
local access/model/configuration/MCP inventory) starts a new conversation; its
old history is inspect-only under the new binding. MCP-only token rotation also
rebootstraps/restores an identical binding, but retains working v1's current
project-access confirmation without an SSO refresh or another write prompt.
Native scope additionally binds per-server MCP grant IDs/catalog revisions absent
from engine equality; re-sign-in/sign-out/removal/revision change retires the
conversation. Expiry alone leaves inference usable with that non-required server
unavailable and native calls denied. This continuity is distinct from inspect-only
imported history. No token or refresh credential enters React, diagnostics, process arguments or
inherited environment. One-time operator-entered inputs remain transient.

Use independent v2 bundle/data/Keychain namespaces. Protected work is encrypted
and account/context-scoped; no plaintext fallback. Reviewed import creates a new
v2 data generation, never modifies v1, transfers no credentials/approvals and
retains a restartable receipt/rollback generation. Historical conversations are
inspect-only until fresh-context compatibility is qualified.

Tests cross native process and identity interfaces: malformed/oversized bootstrap,
queue pressure, wrong context, crash/EOF/graceful and forced cleanup, signature/
issuer/audience/key-rotation rejection, scoped sign-out, protected histories and
Node-free installed inventory. Native/live/final signed-byte proof remain separate.
See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for identity/crypto proposals.
# 4. Managed HTTP enters native configuration authority

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D014,D016 and C14/C15 requirements.

V1's workbench has real request-document and transport depth. Its redactions-based
redirect guard is not explicit tenant-credential classification, Live mode lacks
application destination binding, and its post-401 retry can resend a mutation.
The stricter v2 review/no-replay policy must be enforced below every Send path.

Keep RequestDocument reconciliation separate. Native execution admission captures
a fresh immutable effective request and explicitly distinguishes tenant-managed,
workbench-owned and anonymous credentials. Known managed reads need capability/
destination admission. Known mutations need exact Configuration review. Unknown
managed methods/paths are denied, even if presented as a read or generic draft.
Ordinary workbench-owned/anonymous HTTP remains supported.

Replace the editable SCM collection-ID credential gate with native-owned
capability registration. Imported IDs/references cannot authorize tenant secret
resolution. An edited token_url/request URL is admitted against the explicit
credential destination before secrets are obtained; an ID change never creates
a capability. Preserve operator-reviewed endpoint configuration separately.

Bind review to expanded URL/method/headers/body and file hashes, variables,
cookies, credential/context/destination and resource classification. Recheck
before dispatch; changed input/context/files require fresh review. Rust constructs
the actual request; renderer fields cannot mark it preapproved.

Managed redirects are disabled initially: a 3xx is shown, and following it is a
new prepared/admitted request. Generic redirects preserve bounded cookie/credential
origin policy. No automatic workbench resend follows HTTP401, refresh, reconnect,
lost response, import or history replay. Refresh can precede a new explicit Send.

The built-in catalogue has 193 request specifications, 81 with mutation methods;
this is metadata parity, not a claim of dedicated/live-qualified product support.
Tests cover imported/edited requests, unknown managed endpoints, direct IPC bypass,
ambiguous paths/auth headers, same/cross-origin redirects, stale cookie/file/
variable/context bindings, double Apply and post-401/lost-response no resend.

This is a stricter v2 product contract, not an allegation that v1 failed its own
contract. See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).
# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan

Status: product interview decisions D001–D023 accepted; D024/D025 settle source
terms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent
chunk and phase reviewer. G00/G01 passed; implementation proceeds from C01. A passing plan review is not a passing architecture,
SDK, application, live-tenant, or package review.

## Outcome and exclusions

Deliver a fresh macOS Apple Silicon desktop application with React presentation
in Tauri, a focused Rust application core, direct Rust SCM SDK integration, and
the native Rust Harness engine. Academy supplies shared presentation and remains
the authority for remote learning. Preserve agreed working v1 behavior, using
v1 as requirements reference. Do not translate its GPUI implementation wholesale.

No desktop billing, media-generation backend, local MySQL/Redis server stack,
Node/Bun backend runtime, TypeScript workers, speculative browser client, or
in-app updater is included. Academy gamification remains inside Academy. Gateway
usage/cost/budget/limits remain operational product functionality. Build-time
JavaScript tooling and React WebView JavaScript are expected.

Source destination: `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git`.
Checkout: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.

## Architectural guiding principles

1. Design deep modules: substantial behavior behind a small domain interface.
   Apply the deletion test; do not wrap every SDK method in a pass-through module.
2. The interface is the test surface. Exercise production transitions and
   adapters; use independent wire/protocol expectations for external contracts.
3. One adapter is a hypothetical seam; two real adapters justify a seam. Shared
   Academy presentation has real web/desktop consumers. Do not build speculative
   browser/backend/plugin architectures for this desktop delivery.
4. Rust owns authority and locality for tenant/credential/workspace context,
   request lifecycle, configuration review, protected storage, and approvals.
   React owns presentation and editable input, not permission to execute.
5. The SDK owns its declared transport/authentication contracts. The application
   owns interactive identity, OS storage, context admission, and workflow policy.
   Do not duplicate SDK retry/pagination/OAuth policy without a documented need.
6. Unknown outcomes and partial/stale/denied/unsupported data remain distinct.
   Never map an unknown verdict to allow or a cancelled write to successful rollback.
7. Host credentials and capabilities are explicit. No ambient environment-file
   discovery, inherited production fallback, generic renderer secret access,
   unrestricted native plugin grants, or cross-origin credential forwarding.
8. Shared appearance does not imply shared learner state. Tenant operations,
   Academy identity/progression, Harness inference identity, and MCP grants stay
   separately scoped. A tenant switch does not sign the learner out of Academy.
9. Keep provenance and reproducibility local to dependency selection. Exact
   versions, source hashes, build inputs, notices, and protocol pins are evidence;
   a neighboring active worktree is not a release dependency.
10. Measure native usability/performance and the actual installed package. A
    successful browser render or mocked IPC assertion is insufficient evidence
    for Tauri host behavior, VoiceOver, Keychain, sidecar isolation, or notarization.

Concrete interfaces, crate decomposition, shared package extraction, and storage
formats are outputs of G01. This plan defines behavior and testable work, not
unreviewed Rust signatures or a predetermined hierarchy of tiny modules.

## Ordered phases and chunks

Every Cxx has its own instruction file under [chunks](chunks/README.md). Complete
chunks in numeric order. A dependency delay blocks its chunk; report it honestly
and continue only research/preparation explicitly within that chunk. Do not
silently advance a later phase or replace a real dependency with a fake to pass.
For a prolonged upstream stall, report the exact dependency and offer an owner
reorder/scope decision. Do not wait indefinitely or reorder silently; an approved
change updates predecessors, plan and review evidence before execution.

| Phase | Chunks   | Result                                                           |
|-------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|
| P00   | G00, G01 | Confirmed plan; selected and reviewed architecture               |
| P01   | C01–C03  | Shared desktop shell, native core, first complete Runtime scan   |
| P02   | C04–C07  | Academy identity, learner presentation, assessment, offline work |
| P03   | C08–C12  | Runtime/DLP administration, Sessions, reports, Library           |
| P04   | C13–C15  | Existing Red Team/Model reads and complete API Client workflows  |
| P05   | C16–C18  | Gateway workspace/configuration, reviewed changes, inference     |
| P06   | C19–C22  | Native Harness, Rust sign-in, typed host tools, workspace MCP    |
| P07   | C23–C24  | Reviewed imports, native quality, accessibility/privacy closure  |
| P08   | C25–C26  | Controlled live qualification; signed installed DMG acceptance   |
| P09   | C27–C34  | UI v2 "Refined Native": spec, shell, wizard, destinations (D058) |

### G00 — Plan confirmation

Finish the decision ledger, baseline coverage, chunk instructions, review/test
protocols, dependency gates, and architecture handoff. Obtain an actual independent
plan review meeting D019. Product decisions are accepted and the owner explicitly
selected the architecture candidates and source terms in D024/D025. Continue the
requested design work; no further blanket scope question is needed. Architecture
review and implementation evidence remain distinct gates.

### G01 — Architecture improvement workflow

Follow [the handoff](architecture-handoff.md) and the requested
improve-codebase-architecture skill. Explore the named areas, show a temporary
HTML report with before/after candidates, ask the owner to choose, and grill the
selected design. Produce ADRs, interface/test ownership, dependency/import
decisions, and concrete chunk command specifications. Review the architecture
at least 9/10 in every critical dimension and overall. Revise this plan if the
selected design affects it, then re-review affected planning evidence.

No application scaffolding, shared-source refactoring, real-data migration,
product endpoint calls, or packaging implementation begins before G01 passes.

## Per-chunk execution contract

1. Read AGENTS/CLAUDE, glossary, accepted ADRs, the chunk, predecessor receipts,
   dependency manifest, test catalogue, and its canonical implementer prompt.
2. Confirm a clean approved source snapshot and identify unrelated user work.
   Resolve dependency availability/provenance and baseline check limitations.
3. Implement only that chunk and approved supporting changes. A shared Academy,
   SDK, or Harness change needs its own reviewed revision and regression evidence;
   preserve active work and do not move another project's main branch casually.
4. Run exact checks from the approved command manifest. Use deterministic local
   fixtures and literal external contracts, then the appropriate native tests.
   Never call hosted application endpoints by accident.
5. Record commands, environments, inputs, results, failures, hashes, limitations,
   and rollback rehearsal. Save raw sensitive evidence privately; commit redacted
   summaries with references/hashes, not credentials or customer bodies.
6. Freeze source and dependency inputs. Request actual independent Codex
   review through [the independent prompt](prompts/review-chunk.md) (D026).
7. If any critical dimension/overall score is below 9, required fixes remain,
   source changed, or mandatory evidence is absent: remediate, rerun affected
   checks, freeze a new snapshot, and request a new review of the same chunk.
8. Complete only after the receipt and evidence validate against current inputs.
   After a phase's last chunk, obtain the cumulative Codex phase review
   ([prompt](prompts/review-phase.md)) before starting the next phase.
   Make a focused commit; revalidate that its tree matches the reviewed tree.
   Later edits invalidate affected receipts and trigger targeted regression
   checks/re-review. Preserve failed attempts; never pressure a reviewer to pass.

## Command contract to establish in C01

These commands are a required future interface, not tools that exist today:

```text
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- check --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- test --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- native --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --chunk Cxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --phase Pxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- complete --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind preview --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
cargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind release-candidate --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json
```

C01 creates the controller and a versioned manifest of exact commands, working
directories, targets, features, fixtures, timeouts, and expected evidence. It
cannot implement the later chunks itself. G01 specifies actual Rust/pnpm/Tauri
commands after choosing the workspace; a future engineer must not guess them or
claim these placeholder commands already ran. Document/test the controller's
rejection of missing, stale, malformed, edited, or sub-threshold receipts.

## Coverage and evidence

- [Feature baseline](feature-baseline.md): existing working/preview/stub behavior.
- [Parity matrix](parity-matrix.md): work ownership and release closure.
- [Dependency gates](dependencies.md): accepted inputs and unresolved qualification.
- [Test catalogue](test-catalogue.md): named behavioral/native/artifact test cases.
- [Review protocol](review-protocol.md): scoring, receipt fields, invalidation.
- [Release contract](release-contract.md): live scope, signing, package/install proof.
- [Mandatory live checks](live-checks.md): fixed L-family/F-smoke requirements and
  owner-approved per-workflow live scope; no access-driven silent reductions.
- [Distribution gate](distribution.md): owner-selected source/binary terms,
  Academy permission, framework disposition and exact shipped notices.
- [Status](status.md): authoritative current state; planned is not implemented.

G01 must convert each chunk's named tests into exact invocation commands and
observable assertions. Tests cannot simply repeat implementation decisions.
Fresh signed-artifact checks bind the final DMG hash and installed bytes; source
or fixture checks do not substitute for them.

## Expected sequencing dependencies

The scan proof can use accepted SDK Phase 3 after native macOS qualification.
Management/DLP uses accepted Phase 4 or a later explicitly reviewed compatible
pin. Existing Red Team/Model reads need verified Phase 5 capabilities; complete
Gateway needs Phase 6 capabilities. Harness/Academy transports are application
work, not delivered by the SCM SDK. Keep these upstream dependencies visible and
do not require unrelated SDK operations merely because they share a library.

First-slice metrics establish named budgets before C04. Every UI chunk includes
keyboard/focus checks. Later introducing chunks register their numeric workload
budgets before implementation/acceptance measurement under T20 governance; budget
changes need recorded rationale and owner or independent reviewer acknowledgement.
C24/C26 compare these pre-registered values, not newly fitted limits. C24 closes
the full native/VoiceOver/performance and cross-feature offline matrix.
Preview packages may demonstrate accepted scope but must name missing features.
Only C26 can establish stable installed-package readiness.

